    <node id="FIRE">
      <data key="d0">FIRE</data>
      <data key="d1">FIRE</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">The fire serves as the central source of warmth and light in the room, around which the party gathers, and it also illuminates the characters and sets the mood within the shanty. It was built by Tom's camp in a dark wood by the shore of a creek, where the party sat during the night. Historically, the fire was worshipped by the Mayas, Chaldeans, and Hindoos, but not by the Egyptians, who regarded it as an animal that devours all things.
Relationships:
  - Source: TOTO
  - Target: FIRE
  - Description: Toto is part of the gathering around the fire, indicating proximity.
  - Source: BRUIN
  - Target: FIRE
  - Description: Bruin sits by the fire, blinking with sober pleasure.
  - Source: GRANDMOTHER
  - Target: FIRE
  - Description: The grandmother is part of the gathering around the fire.
  - Source: RACCOON
  - Target: FIRE
  - Description: The raccoon is part of the gathering around the fire, indicating proximity.
  - Source: SQUIRREL
  - Target: FIRE
  - Description: The squirrel is part of the gathering around the fire, indicating proximity.
  - Source: PARROT
  - Target: FIRE
  - Description: The parrot is part of the gathering around the fire.
  - Source: WOODCHUCK
  - Target: FIRE
  - Description: The woodchuck is in a cupboard near the fire, but asleep.
  - Source: WOOD-PIGEON
  - Target: FIRE
  - Description: The wood-pigeon is part of the gathering around the fire, indicating proximity.
  - Source: REBELLION OF 1837
  - Target: FIRE
  - Description: Fire was used during the rebellion
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: FIRE
  - Description: Naso will survive the closing fire, as a great portion of himself will live on.
  - Source: JOHN A. LASCO
  - Target: FIRE
  - Description: John A. Lasco is injured in the fire on the Brandywine.
  - Source: SUZA FAIRFIELD
  - Target: FIRE
  - Description: Suza begged Tom to let her come to the fire, indicating a relationship of seeking warmth and comfort.
  - Source: CHURCH
  - Target: FIRE
  - Description: Fire was lit behind the altar of the church
  - Source: LADY
  - Target: FIRE
  - Description: The lady draws nearer to the fire and compliments its brightness and comfort
  - Source: TOM DITAMUS
  - Target: FIRE
  - Description: Tom encamped for the night and built a fire by the side of a large log, indicating a relationship of use for warmth and light.
  - Source: SOPHY
  - Target: FIRE
  - Description: Sophy stands near the fire, and her appearance is illuminated by the firelight
  - Source: PEOPLE'S THEATER
  - Target: FIRE
  - Description: The People's theater burned down during Colfax's speech.
  - Source: FIRE
  - Target: FIREPLACE
  - Description: The fire is in the fireplace.
  - Source: POESY
  - Target: FIRE
  - Description: Even when the closing fire consumes the poet, a great portion of himself will survive.
  - Source: BRANDYWINE
  - Target: FIRE
  - Description: The Brandywine caught fire, leading to its destruction and the deaths of many passengers.
  - Source: ALTAR
  - Target: FIRE
  - Description: Fire was lit behind the altar</data>
