    <node id="MUSSELMEN">
      <data key="d0">MUSSELMEN</data>
      <data key="d1">MUSSELMEN</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">MUSSELMEN, also referred to as Mussulmen, are a group of Muslims distinguished by their deep veneration of the Shereefs—descendants of the Prophet Muhammad—and their profound reverence for Abd-el-Kader, the famed 19th-century Algerian religious and military leader. This devotion places them within a specific Islamic cultural and spiritual tradition, emphasizing respect for holy lineages and historical figures of resistance.

In addition to their religious practices, the Musselmen are noted for their encounters with enchantresses in the Argan forests, where they were reportedly pestered or harassed by these supernatural or mystical beings. This experience suggests a belief in or interaction with the spiritual or folkloric world, which is woven into their daily lives and narratives.

Furthermore, the Musselmen engaged in discussions about slavery with the narrator of the text. This indicates that they were participants in broader social and ethical conversations, likely reflecting the complex historical contexts of their time, including issues of bondage, freedom, and human dignity. Their willingness to discuss such topics reveals a community engaged with moral and societal questions.

Overall, the Musselmen are portrayed as a devout Muslim community with strong ties to religious authority figures, a rich folkloric tradition involving enchantresses, and a history of engaging in serious discourse on social issues. Their identity is shaped by a blend of orthodox Islamic devotion, local legends, and active participation in contemporary debates.
