    <node id="NASO">
      <data key="d0">NASO</data>
      <data key="d1">NASO</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Naso is the poet and narrator of the text, a figure who serves as the central voice and author of the elegies. He addresses his mistress, Corinna, and frequently offers gifts and inscriptions at altars, dedicating his tablets to Venus in the context of his love experiences. Born among the Peligni, Naso composes elegies that explore themes of love, personal failings, and the influence of Cupid and the Muses on his work. He is also the speaker who makes offerings to the gods for Corinna's preservation, acting as both the creator and the protagonist of his poetic narrative.
Relationships:
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: LIGHTNINGS
  - Description: Naso forsook the lightnings of Jove when his mistress shut her door.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: BATTLE OF THE HEAVENS
  - Description: Naso initially attempted to write about the battle of the heavens but abandoned it for love poetry.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: JOVE
  - Description: Naso forsook Jove and his lightnings when his mistress shut her door, abandoning the epic on the giants.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: POESY
  - Description: Naso defends poesy as exempt from death and the source of everlasting fame.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: ROME
  - Description: Naso refers to Rome as the ruler of the conquered earth, whose fame is tied to the endurance of poetry.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: CORINNA
  - Description: Naso addresses Corinna as his mistress, offers her gifts, and gives her advice about her actions, indicating a close romantic relationship.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: CUPID
  - Description: Naso is compelled by Cupid to write love poetry instead of epic wars, as Cupid demands his work.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: VENUS
  - Description: Naso dedicates his tablets to Venus and plans to place them in her temple, showing his devotion and offering to the goddess of love.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: APOLLO
  - Description: Naso wishes for Apollo to supply him with cups from the Castalian stream, seeking divine inspiration.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: TIBULLUS
  - Description: Naso cites Tibullus as an example of polished poetry that will be repeated as long as Cupid's arms endure.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: ÆNEAS
  - Description: Naso cites Æneas (from Virgil) as an example of poetry that will be read as long as Rome rules.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: MISTRESS
  - Description: Naso's mistress shut her door against him, causing him to abandon his epic and turn to love poetry.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: GALLUS
  - Description: Naso cites Gallus as an example of a poet known in both West and East.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: AURORA
  - Description: Naso directly addresses Aurora, criticizing her for hastening the morning and disturbing his time with his mistress, showing a clear adversarial relationship.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: ARATUS
  - Description: Naso cites Aratus as an example of immortal poetry about the heavens.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: DEATH
  - Description: Naso will live on after death, as poetry is immortal.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: TITHONUS
  - Description: Naso wishes Tithonus could tell tales about Aurora, implying a shared grievance against her, though they are not directly connected otherwise.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: MENANDER
  - Description: Naso cites Menander as an example of immortal comic poetry.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: IDA
  - Description: Naso mentions Ida as a landmark that will endure as long as Homer's fame.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: OLYMPUS
  - Description: Naso mentions Olympus in the context of the giants' battle, which he abandoned.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: GYGES
  - Description: Naso mentions Gyges in the context of the giants' battle, which he abandoned.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: EARTH
  - Description: Naso mentions Earth as avenging herself in the giants' battle, which he abandoned.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: HUSBAND
  - Description: Naso sees the husband as an obstacle, showing a relationship of rivalry.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: VARRO
  - Description: Naso cites Varro as an example of a poet whose works on the first ship and golden fleece are immortal.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: TENEDOS
  - Description: Naso mentions Tenedos as a landmark that will endure as long as Homer's fame.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: PELION
  - Description: Naso mentions Pelion in the context of the giants' battle, which he abandoned.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: TAGUS
  - Description: Naso mentions the Tagus as a wealthy shore that yields to poetry.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: RING
  - Description: Naso presents the ring to Corinna and expresses a wish to become the ring, showing his deep affection and desire for closeness.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: ÆÆA
  - Description: Naso references the arts of Ææa as a potential means to transform himself into a ring, indicating a connection to the magical place.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: LATINUS
  - Description: Naso references Latinus as an example of female-caused conflict, showing a relationship of historical analogy.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: SERPENT
  - Description: Naso entreats by the serpent, showing a relationship of supplication.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: APIS
  - Description: Naso entreats by Apis, showing a relationship of supplication.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: NAPE
  - Description: Napè is a servant in the household of Naso's mistress, and her stumble is seen as an omen by Naso, linking her to his misfortunes.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: SOPHOCLES
  - Description: Naso cites Sophocles as an example of immortal tragic poetry.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: PELIGNI
  - Description: Naso was born among the Peligni, a people in Italy, and identifies himself as their poet.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: MÆONIAN BARD
  - Description: Naso cites the Mæonian bard (Homer) as an example of immortal poetic fame.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: ENNIUS
  - Description: Naso cites Ennius as an example of a poet whose name will not perish.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: LUCRETIUS
  - Description: Naso cites Lucretius as an example of a sublime poet whose verses will last until the world's end.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: TITYRUS
  - Description: Naso cites Tityrus (from Virgil) as an example of poetry that will be read as long as Rome rules.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: SIMOIS
  - Description: Naso mentions Simois as a river that will flow as long as Homer's fame.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: OSSA
  - Description: Naso mentions Ossa in the context of the giants' battle, which he abandoned.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: SISTRA
  - Description: Naso entreats by the sistra, showing a relationship of supplication.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: ILITHYIA
  - Description: Naso entreats Ilithyia for aid, showing a relationship of supplication.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: ENVY
  - Description: Envy blames Naso for idleness and criticizes his poetic pursuits, but Naso defends poetry as immortal.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: ASCRÆAN
  - Description: Naso cites the Ascræan (Hesiod) as an example of immortal poetic fame.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: WARS OF THE GIANTS
  - Description: Naso initially attempted to sing of the wars of the giants but abandoned them for love poetry.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: VICTIM
  - Description: Naso promises a victim, showing a relationship of vow.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: SAND-HEAP
  - Description: Naso plans to heap sand, showing a relationship of preparation.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: LUCIFER
  - Description: Naso hopes Lucifer brings the day, showing a relationship of hope.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: SON OF ATREUS
  - Description: Naso references him as a comparison, showing a relationship of analogy.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: DAUGHTER OF TYNDARUS
  - Description: Naso references her, showing a relationship of historical analogy.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: TROJANS
  - Description: Naso references the Trojans as an example of female-caused conflict, showing a relationship of historical analogy.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: CASTALIAN STREAM
  - Description: Naso desires to drink from the Castalian stream, a source of poetic inspiration.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: SON OF BATTUS
  - Description: Naso cites the son of Battus (Callimachus) as an example of immortal poetic fame.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: ACCIUS
  - Description: Naso cites Accius as an example of a poet with spirited language whose name will not perish.
  - Source: NASO
  - Target: LYCORIS
  - Description: Naso mentions Lycoris as Gallus's beloved, whose name will be known with his.

