      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">My Father is a recurring figure in the diary of Samuel Pepys, serving as both a personal relative and a participant in family and business affairs. He is identified as Samuel Pepys's father, and his activities are closely tied to the Pepys family’s domestic, financial, and legal matters.

In his role within the household, My Father is given orders regarding the purchase of cloth for "My Lord," indicating his involvement in procurement and service to the Pepys patron. He also assists in the broader business of buying cloth and is engaged in related transactions, suggesting a practical, hands-on role in trade or household management.

My Father frequently dines with Samuel Pepys, including a notable meal on the 18th with Dr. Tom Pepys, another family member. These gatherings reflect his active participation in family social life and his close relationship with his son.

Beyond daily affairs, My Father is deeply involved in estate and legal matters. He travels to Brampton, where he settles estate issues and later returns from there to discuss business. He is present during the reading of the Captain's will and participates in discussions about mourning expenses, indicating his role in executing family obligations and managing inheritance. He is also involved in family discussions about Pall (likely a relative) and financial matters, and he employs a servant named Ned, showing his position as a head of household.

My Father is considered for a position in the Wardrobe, a royal or noble household department, which highlights his standing and the family’s connections. His plans to go to the country are frequently mentioned, reflecting his mobility between London and rural estates.

Throughout these descriptions, My Father emerges as a central figure in the Pepys family narrative: a trusted agent for business transactions, a participant in legal and financial decisions, a social companion to his son, and a manager of domestic staff. His actions are consistently tied to family cohesion, estate administration, and the practicalities of daily life in 17th-century England. The descriptions are consistent and non-contradictory, painting a portrait of a diligent, involved patriarch whose life is interwoven with Samuel Pepys's own diary entries.
