    <node id="THE COOLIES">
      <data key="d0">THE COOLIES</data>
      <data key="d1">THE COOLIES</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">The Coolies are native laborers depicted in the context of a colonial-era compound and ship, serving multiple roles across the narrative. On land, they are described as squatting figures who are ever-present around the compound, often seen sleeping on the grass at night. Their primary duty involves attending to Rudolph's pony: they spring up from their resting positions to hold the animal when Rudolph appears, and they react to his presence with cackling laughter, which serves as a local, informal commentary on his arrival. This behavior highlights their role as both practical servants and spontaneous observers of the social scene.

Aboard the captain's ship, the Coolies take on a different function. Due to an outbreak of plague, two of them are stowed topside, hidden under the boats, as a precautionary measure to isolate them from the rest of the crew and passengers. This placement reflects the harsh realities of disease control in the period, where laborers were segregated for the safety of others, often in cramped and exposed conditions.

Overall, the Coolies are portrayed as a marginalized but integral part of the environment—whether on land or at sea—performing menial tasks, enduring precarious living situations, and providing a glimpse into the social dynamics and racial hierarchies of the time. Their presence is constant, yet their agency is limited, as they are defined by their labor and the reactions they elicit from the central characters. The descriptions collectively paint a picture of a group whose lives are intertwined with the daily operations of the compound and the ship, yet who remain on the periphery, subject to the whims and needs of those they serve.
Relationships:
  - Source: RUDOLPH
  - Target: THE COOLIES
  - Description: RUDOLPH is a figure surrounded by a group of local attendants known as THE COOLIES. These individuals are consistently depicted as holding Rudolph's pony, which establishes a clear service relationship between them and Rudolph. Their presence around him is not passive; they actively react to his appearance, demonstrating a level of local engagement and responsiveness to his arrival or actions. The Coolies serve as both practical support—managing the pony—and as a social presence that acknowledges Rudolph, indicating that he operates within a context of local assistance and communal awareness. There is no contradiction in the descriptions; rather, they complement each other, painting a picture of Rudolph as a person of some standing or importance, attended to by a group of local workers who are both functional and observant in their duties.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
