    <node id="KUKULCAN">
      <data key="d0">KUKULCAN</data>
      <data key="d1">KUKULCAN</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">KUKULCAN is a significant deity in Mesoamerican mythology, primarily revered by the Maya civilization. He is most commonly identified as the "winged serpent" or feathered serpent, a powerful and multifaceted figure whose influence extended across various cultures and time periods. In Mayan tradition, Kukulcan is closely associated with the sanctuaries and temples built by rulers, and his image is prominently featured in Mayan art and architecture, symbolizing divine authority and cosmic order.

Beyond his role as a deity, Kukulcan is also celebrated as a culture hero among the Maya. He is credited with bringing knowledge, civilization, and cultural practices to the people, embodying the ideals of wisdom and leadership. His worship was not confined to the Maya alone; he was also venerated by other Mesoamerican groups under different names. The Mexicans (Aztecs) knew him as Quetzalcoatl, while the Quiché Maya referred to him as Cucumatz. This cross-cultural recognition highlights his widespread importance and the shared mythological heritage of the region.

There is also a speculative connection between Kukulcan and Chaacmol, a figure associated with the rain god Chaac. Some traditions suggest that Kukulcan may have been an ancestor or a manifestation of Chaacmol, though this link is less clearly defined and may represent a later syncretic development. Despite these variations, the core identity of Kukulcan as the feathered serpent remains consistent, symbolizing the union of the earthly (serpent) and the celestial (bird), and serving as a bridge between the human and divine realms.

In summary, Kukulcan is a central figure in Maya mythology, embodying both divine and heroic qualities. He is the winged serpent whose sanctuaries and artistic representations reflect his sacred status, and whose legacy as a culture hero extends beyond the Maya to other Mesoamerican civilizations. His multiple names across cultures—Quetzalcoatl among the Aztecs and Cucumatz among the Quiché—underscore his universal appeal and enduring significance in the religious and cultural history of ancient Mesoamerica.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.</data>
