    <node id="IZAMAL">
      <data key="d0">IZAMAL</data>
      <data key="d1">IZAMAL</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Izamal is a significant historical and archaeological city located in the Yucatán region of Mexico, deeply tied to ancient Maya religious and funerary practices. The city is most notably associated with the worship of the goddess Moo, who was venerated both during her lifetime and after her death. According to colonial-era sources such as Cogolludo, Lizana, and Landa, Moo was later deified and worshipped under the name Kinich-Kakmo, a solar deity whose cult remained active up to the time of the Spanish conquest. This dual identity—Moo and Kinich-Kakmo—reflects the city’s central role in Maya cosmology and ritual life.

Izamal is also the site of several important shrines and temples. One of these, the shrine of Moo, was constructed on a high mound on the north side of the city’s large central square, indicating its prominence in public worship. Another key structure, the shrine of Kabul, was dedicated to an idol bearing a hand symbol, as documented by both Cogolludo and Lizana. These temples and mounds were not only places of devotion but also served as burial sites; archaeological findings have revealed human remains interred in urns within the city’s mounds, pointing to complex funerary customs and the practice of ancestor veneration.

The city’s religious significance is further underscored by its long-standing worship of Kinich-Kakmo, which persisted until the arrival of Spanish colonizers. This continuity of worship suggests that Izamal was a major pilgrimage and ceremonial center for centuries. The combination of monumental architecture, dedicated shrines, and evidence of human burial in urns indicates that Izamal functioned as both a sacred landscape and a community hub for ritual activity. The hand symbol associated with the Kabul shrine may represent a specific deity or a form of divine communication, adding another layer to the city’s rich symbolic heritage.

Overall, Izamal stands out as a key Maya city where mythology, architecture, and mortuary practices intertwined. Its association with Moo and Kinich-Kakmo, along with the physical remains of temples and urns, provides a comprehensive picture of a vibrant religious center that played a crucial role in the spiritual life of the Yucatán Peninsula before and during the early colonial period.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.</data>
