    <node id="AAC">
      <data key="d0">AAC</data>
      <data key="d1">AAC</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">AAC is a figure from ancient Maya mythology and monumental art, depicted as the brother of Chaacmol and Moo. According to the available descriptions, Aac’s primary narrative role is that of a fratricidal sibling, driven by jealousy over Moo. The accounts consistently state that Aac killed Chaacmol, but they vary slightly in the method and detail of the murder. One description specifies that Aac killed Chaacmol with three spear stabs in the back, while another notes that he stabbed him three times, as depicted in the monuments. A third description simply states that Aac killed Chaacmol out of jealousy over Moo, without specifying the weapon or number of wounds. These details are not contradictory but rather complementary, with the more specific accounts providing the exact manner of death—three spear thrusts to the back—while the broader description focuses on the motive. Thus, the unified account is that Aac, the brother of Chaacmol and Moo, murdered Chaacmol by stabbing him three times in the back with a spear, an act motivated by his jealousy over Moo. This event is recorded in monumental art, indicating its significance in the mythological or historical record. Aac’s actions define him as a tragic and morally complex figure, whose jealousy led to the violent death of his own brother. The narrative does not provide further details about Aac’s fate, his relationship with Moo after the murder, or any subsequent consequences, leaving his story as a stark example of destructive sibling rivalry. The consistency across the descriptions confirms that Aac is a central character in this mythic episode, and the inclusion of the murder in monuments underscores its cultural importance. In summary, Aac is a brother who, consumed by jealousy over Moo, killed Chaacmol with three spear stabs to the back, a deed immortalized in ancient carvings.
Relationships: None listed in the provided data.</data>
