    <node id="KING MARK">
      <data key="d0">KING MARK</data>
      <data key="d1">KING MARK</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">King Mark is a legendary figure from Arthurian and Celtic tradition, primarily known as the King of Cornwall. He is closely associated with the coastal stronghold of Tintagel, which is often cited as his primary seat of power and a significant landing place in his lore. In the medieval romances, Mark is depicted as the uncle of the renowned knight Sir Tristram (also known as Tristan) and the husband of the beautiful Irish princess Iseult (or Isolde). His story is most famously intertwined with the tragic love triangle between himself, Tristram, and Iseult, a narrative that explores themes of loyalty, betrayal, and chivalric duty. While some sources emphasize his role as a king and uncle, others highlight his status as a legendary Cornish hero, with Tintagel serving as a symbolic and geographical anchor for his reign. The descriptions consistently place him in Cornwall, with Tintagel being a recurring and possibly legendary landing-place rather than a confirmed historical site. Despite variations in emphasis—whether as a king, a hero, or a figure tied to a specific location—the core identity of King Mark remains that of a Cornish ruler whose legacy is inseparable from the tragic romance of Tristram and Iseult. His character is often portrayed with complexity, sometimes as a jealous and vengeful husband, yet also as a figure of authority and tragedy within the broader Arthurian world. The association with Tintagel, though possibly more mythic than historical, reinforces his connection to the rugged Cornish coastline and the enduring power of Celtic legend. Overall, King Mark stands as a pivotal figure in medieval literature, embodying the tensions between personal desire, political duty, and familial bonds.
Relationships:
  - Source: KING UTHER
  - Target: KING MARK
  - Description: Both are Cornish heroes associated with Tintagel

---</data>
