    <node id="MR. FALCONER">
      <data key="d0">MR. FALCONER</data>
      <data key="d1">MR. FALCONER</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Mr. Falconer, a resident of Woolwich, is a character encountered by the narrator in two distinct social settings. In one instance, he acts as a generous host, offering the narrator cherries and good wine, suggesting a warm and hospitable nature. In another, he is described as being made drunk by the narrator and his companions at a place called the Dolphin, indicating that he participates in convivial, perhaps boisterous, gatherings. These two descriptions, while seemingly contrasting—one of refined hospitality and the other of overindulgence—are not contradictory but rather illustrate different facets of his social interactions. Together, they portray Mr. Falconer as a sociable individual who engages in both gracious hosting and lively, potentially excessive, drinking with friends. His identity is tied to Woolwich, and his experiences with the narrator highlight a relationship built on shared meals, drink, and companionship.
**Relationships:**
  - Source: MR. FALCONER
  - Target: NARRATOR
  - Description: Mr. Falconer gives the narrator cherries and wine, showing hospitality.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
