    </node>
    <node id="EMPEROR">
      <data key="d0">EMPEROR</data>
      <data key="d1">EMPEROR</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">His Celestial Majesty, the Emperor, is the supreme ruler of the Chinese Empire, issuing the death sentence for Ly-Chee after the latter is drenched. In this court, a person arriving without a present is considered a menace, as a gift serves as a formal greeting. The Emperor also lit a lighthouse for the first time, and his candle was kept as a relic by the main character.

Separately, the Emperor is identified as the Sultan of Morocco, whose authority is now largely nominal. He is the ruler to whom the Moorish Deputy-Governor owed duty, and he held the British Consul and others liable for certain actions. The Emperor of Morocco was involved in hostilities with the French, driven by fanatics preaching "the holy war" from Fez. Merchants are allegedly indebted to him, and he holds final judge powers under treaties. In a deputation, presents would have been given to him, as he is the ultimate authority in the country. His palace in Mequinez is simple and elegant, featuring Arabesque stucco work. He resides for a third of his time at Morocco, with the rest spent at Fez and Mequinez, traveling to awe tribes and excite loyalty for the Shereefian throne. The Emperor feels ill at ease when priests appear abroad and employs arts to get them to retire, promising to be guided by their councils.

These descriptions refer to two distinct rulers: the Emperor of the Chinese Empire (His Celestial Majesty) and the Emperor of Morocco (the Sultan). The Chinese Emperor is an absolute authority who orders executions and values ceremonial gifts, while the Moroccan Emperor, though nominally powerful, faces challenges from French hostilities and internal religious fanaticism. Both are ultimate authorities in their respective realms, with the Moroccan Emperor maintaining a mobile court to consolidate power and the Chinese Emperor presiding over a rigid court protocol. The contradictions between the two are resolved by recognizing they are separate entities sharing the title "Emperor."
Relationships:
  - Source: SULTAN
  - Target: EMPEROR
  - Description: SULTAN and EMPEROR are titles of sovereign authority, often used interchangeably in certain historical and cultural contexts. The Sultan is also referred to as the Emperor of Morocco, indicating that in this specific region, the two titles denote the same supreme ruler, combining Islamic and imperial connotations. In courtly tradition, a person who approaches the Emperor without offering a present is considered a menace or a source of discord, as the presentation of a gift functions as a formal greeting and a sign of respect. Thus, the act of giving is not merely ceremonial but essential to maintaining proper diplomatic and social relations with the sovereign. Together, these descriptions highlight the dual nature of the ruler—both as a Sultan with religious and political authority and as an Emperor with imperial status—and underscore the importance of gift-giving as a fundamental protocol in his court.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: EMPEROR
Description: His Celestial Majesty, the Emperor, is the supreme ruler of the Chinese Empire, issuing the death sentence for Ly-Chee after the latter is drenched. In this court, a person arriving without a present is considered a menace, as a gift serves as a formal greeting. The Emperor also lit a lighthouse for the first time, and his candle was kept as a relic by the main character.

Separately, the Emperor is identified as the Sultan of Morocco, whose authority is now largely nominal. He is the ruler to whom the Moorish Deputy-Governor owed duty, and he held the British Consul and others liable for certain actions. The Emperor of Morocco was involved in hostilities with the French, driven by fanatics preaching "the holy war" from Fez. Merchants are allegedly indebted to him, and he holds final judge powers under treaties. In a deputation, presents would have been given to him, as he is the ultimate authority in the country. His palace in Mequinez is simple and elegant, featuring Arabesque stucco work. He resides for a third of his time at Morocco, with the rest spent at Fez and Mequinez, traveling to awe tribes and excite loyalty for the Shereefian throne. The Emperor feels ill at ease when priests appear abroad and employs arts to get them to retire, promising to be guided by their councils.

These descriptions refer to two distinct rulers: the Emperor of the Chinese Empire (His Celestial Majesty) and the Emperor of Morocco (the Sultan). The Chinese Emperor is an absolute authority who orders executions and values ceremonial gifts, while the Moroccan Emperor, though nominally powerful, faces challenges from French hostilities and internal religious fanaticism. Both are ultimate authorities in their respective realms, with the Moroccan Emperor maintaining a mobile court to consolidate power and the Chinese Emperor presiding over a rigid court protocol. The contradictions between the two are resolved by recognizing they are separate entities sharing the title "Emperor."
Relationships:
  - Source: SULTAN
  - Target: EMPEROR
  - Description: SULTAN and EMPEROR are titles of sovereign authority, often used interchangeably in certain historical and cultural contexts. The Sultan is also referred to as the Emperor of Morocco, indicating that in this specific region, the two titles denote the same supreme ruler, combining Islamic and imperial connotations. In courtly tradition, a person who approaches the Emperor without offering a present is considered a menace or a source of discord, as the presentation of a gift functions as a formal greeting and a sign of respect. Thus, the act of giving is not merely ceremonial but essential to maintaining proper diplomatic and social relations with the sovereign. Together, these descriptions highlight the dual nature of the ruler—both as a Sultan with religious and political authority and as an Emperor with imperial status—and underscore the importance of gift-giving as a fundamental protocol in his court.</data>
