    <node id="FEZ">
      <data key="d0">FEZ</data>
      <data key="d1">FEZ</data>
      <data key="d2">endpoint</data>
      <data key="d3">Fez, also known as Fas, is a major city and historical kingdom in northern Morocco, serving as one of the country’s imperial capitals alongside Morocco (Marrakesh) and Mequinez (Meknes). Founded around A.D. 807 by Muley Edris (Edris-Ben-Edris), it was the capital of the Edristees dynasty and later became a center of commerce, administration, learning, and culture. The city is located in the Central Region of the Atlas, with the Atlas ridge reaching its greatest heights to the southeast, and is separated from other parts of Morocco by the Bouragrag River. Fez is also the name of a province and a historical kingdom, and it is a valley in Tafilett that pays impost to the Emperor.

Historically, Fez was a royal city where the Sultan Almansor ruled, and it was one of the cities where the Emperor resided part-time. It was also the site of the 1801 treaty signing. The city was a hub of trade, with commercial relations extending to the Figheegh oases, the Saharan people, and coastal regions like Rabat. It hosted two annual caravans—one to Timbuctoo and one to Mecca—and was known for manufacturing, including the production of the red caps called fez. Fez was a center of arts and learning, with numerous mosques, a university, and a diverse ethnic population. When united with Mequinez, its population was estimated at about one hundred thousand, though other estimates range from 40,000 to 50,000. It had seven gates, two castles, and was surrounded by a decaying wall, with the great square Al-Kaisseriah as a notable site.

Politically, Fez was a center of power and intrigue. It was home to a war party, and British influence was exerted there to neutralize it. The city contained a disaffected population with adherents of Abd-el-Kader. Sidi Mohammed served as Governor, and one of the Emperor’s harems was located there. Muley Suleiman was saluted as Emperor in Fez, which was also the location of Zawiat-Muley-Driss. Despite its grandeur, Fez was known for its fanaticism and rivalry with Morocco city. It was a major Mahometan capital, with fabled origins and historical significance noted by various historians.
Relationships:
  - Source: FIGHEEGH
  - Target: FEZ
  - Description: Figheegh has commercial relations with Fez.
  - Source: TIJIJET
  - Target: FEZ
  - Description: Tijijet is along the route from Fez to Morocco</data>
    </node>
