      <data key="d3">Sir Thomas Malory is the author of *Le Morte d'Arthur* (also referred to as *Morte d'Arthur*), a seminal work that serves as a key source of Arthurian legend. His text is explicitly mentioned as part of the literary provender—that is, the foundational material or intellectual nourishment—drawn upon in the broader narrative. Malory’s compilation is recognized as a crucial literary source, providing the primary account of King Arthur, the Knights of the Round Table, and their associated tales. The work is consistently cited as a definitive reference for Arthurian stories, and Malory is credited with shaping and preserving these legends for later audiences. His authorship is central to the text’s significance, as *Le Morte d'Arthur* is not only a historical artifact but also a creative retelling that has influenced countless subsequent adaptations. In all references, Malory is identified as the sole author of this foundational work, and no contradictions exist among the descriptions; they uniformly affirm his role as the writer of this essential Arthurian text.
