    <node id="TRISTIA">
      <data key="d0">TRISTIA</data>
      <data key="d1">TRISTIA</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">TRISTIA is a literary work by the Roman poet Ovid, referenced extensively across various contexts for its historical, biographical, and cultural details. The text serves as a primary source for notes on the starting-place of the Circus, providing an account of the 'meta' (the turning posts) within the Circus, and also offers a similar description of Alexandria, suggesting comparative urban or architectural observations. It is cited as a source for the life of Ovid himself, indicating that the poet served as a 'judex selectus' (a commissioned judge), a detail that sheds light on his civic roles. Additionally, the work is referenced in relation to Macer, a contemporary or associate of Ovid, and provides details on the 'lustrum' (a ceremonial purification or census period). The Tristia is also noted for a mention of Corinna, a figure who appears in Ovid's poetry, likely as a lover or poetic subject. Overall, the Tristia functions as a multifaceted reference point, offering insights into Roman public spectacles, urban geography, Ovid's personal biography, and his literary circle, while also serving as a general source for various historical and cultural practices.
Relationships: (none listed in the provided data)</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
