      <data key="d3">Sir Tristram Mardon is a multifaceted character who appears in a narrative blending social comedy, horse racing, and romantic subplots, with some descriptions also referencing a legendary namesake. In the primary, contemporary setting, Sir Tristram is a gentleman of authority and a close friend of the Dean, having been his contemporary at Oxford. He is a part-owner of the racehorse Dandy Dick, which is stabled at the Deanery, and he is staying at the local "Swan" inn during the racing season.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">
Description: Sir Tristram Mardon is a multifaceted character who appears in a narrative blending social comedy, horse racing, and romantic subplots, with some descriptions also referencing a legendary namesake. In the primary, contemporary setting, Sir Tristram is a gentleman of authority and a close friend of the Dean, having been his contemporary at Oxford. He is a part-owner of the racehorse Dandy Dick, which is stabled at the Deanery, and he is staying at the local "Swan" inn during the racing season.

Sir Tristram is deeply involved in the central plot, which revolves around a misunderstanding involving the Dean, who is mistakenly secured in a stable. He plays a key role in clarifying this confusion, questioning the servant Hatcham about the man held there, and ultimately showing compassion by refusing to press charges against the Dean. He also assists Georgiana, the Dean's relative, in her rescue efforts, helping her bring Dandy Dick to the Deanery stables after the horse is saved from a fire. In a separate incident, he rushes in breathlessly after rescuing the horse from the flames, demonstrating his quick action and care for the animal.

Beyond his practical assistance, Sir Tristram is a figure of social warmth and affection. He enters scenes quietly, often smoking and carrying a candle, and interacts with Georgiana with concern and tenderness. He is evidently in love with her, and their relationship is intertwined with discussions of horse racing and betting on Dandy Dick. He also raises Hatcham's wages, indicating a generous and fair-minded nature. As a friend of the Dean, he is a regular presence at the Deanery, and his authority is evident when he intervenes with officers and other officials on Georgiana's behalf.

In a separate, mythological context, the name "Sir Tristram" also refers to a knight of the Round Table, nephew of King Mark, who was famously sent to fetch Iseult of Ireland for his uncle, only to fall in love with her himself, and who later fled to Brittany. This legendary figure shares only the name with the modern character, who is instead a pragmatic, kind-hearted gentleman involved in a domestic and equestrian comedy of errors. The modern Sir Tristram is defined by his loyalty, his romantic interest in Georgiana, and his pivotal role in resolving the Dean's predicament with both humor and grace.
Relationships:
  - Source: SIR TRISTRAM
  - Target: THE QUEEN
  - Description: Sir Tristram compares Georgiana to a Queen. Sir Tristram compares Georgiana to a Queen in a Palace.
  - Source: SIR TRISTRAM
  - Target: ISEULT
  - Description: Sir Tristram and Iseult fell in love due to a love-potion, leading to a tragic affair.
  - Source: SIR TRISTRAM
  - Target: BRAGSWAINE
  - Description: Bragswaine gave the love-potion to Sir Tristram and Iseult.
  - Source: SIR TRISTRAM
  - Target: BRITTANY
  - Description: Sir Tristram fled to Brittany and married there.
  - Source: SIR TRISTRAM
  - Target: ISEULT OF THE WHITE HANDS
  - Description: Sir Tristram married Iseult of the White Hands in Brittany.

