    <node id="THETIS">
      <data key="d0">THETIS</data>
      <data key="d1">THETIS</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Thetis is a sea Goddess in Greek mythology, specifically associated with the waves and the ocean. She is the wife of Peleus, the king of Phthia, and together they are the parents of the great hero Achilles. Thetis is also noted for her divine power and foresight, as she is referenced in the text as a figure who could have refused to bear her son, Achilles, thereby altering the course of the Trojan War. Her role as a mother and a goddess of the sea underscores her significance in both divine and mortal affairs.
Relationships:
  - Source: THETIS
  - Target: ACHILLES
  - Description: Thetis is the mother of Achilles, and her refusal to bear him would have prevented his existence, as referenced in the text.
  - Source: THETIS
  - Target: PELEUS
  - Description: Thetis, a sea Goddess, married Peleus, the king of Phthia.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: THETIS
Description: Thetis is a sea Goddess in Greek mythology, specifically associated with the waves and the ocean. She is the wife of Peleus, the king of Phthia, and together they are the parents of the great hero Achilles. Thetis is also noted for her divine power and foresight, as she is referenced in the text as a figure who could have refused to bear her son, Achilles, thereby altering the course of the Trojan War. Her role as a mother and a goddess of the sea underscores her significance in both divine and mortal affairs.
Relationships:
  - Source: THETIS
  - Target: ACHILLES
  - Description: Thetis is the mother of Achilles, and her refusal to bear him would have prevented his existence, as referenced in the text.
  - Source: THETIS
