    </node>
    <node id="ACHILLES">
      <data key="d0">ACHILLES</data>
      <data key="d1">ACHILLES</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Achilles is a central figure in Greek mythology and epic poetry, most notably as the protagonist of Homer’s *Iliad*, which recounts his wrath during the Trojan War. He is celebrated as a swift and formidable Greek hero, whose actions and emotions drive much of the narrative. His deep love for the captive Briseis, a bondwoman of Thessalian origin, inflames his anger and leads to his withdrawal from battle, a pivotal event in the war. This passion also serves as an example of a monarch’s attachment to a servant, illustrating his complex, human vulnerabilities.

Achilles’ story is marked by several key relationships and events. His mother, the sea nymph Thetis, lamented his fate, aware of his impending death, and his birth was considered crucial to the war’s outcome. In addition to his martial prowess, Achilles is credited with a magical spear that healed the wounded hero Telephus, demonstrating his connection to both destruction and restoration. His legacy also includes a famous conflict with Ajax Telamon: after Achilles’ death, his divine armor was awarded to Odysseus rather than Ajax, an insult that drove Ajax to madness and eventual suicide.

Throughout these accounts, Achilles is consistently portrayed as a Greek hero of the Trojan War, whose swiftness, strength, and intense emotions—whether love, grief, or rage—define his character. His story intertwines themes of pride, honor, and mortality, making him one of the most enduring figures in classical literature.
Relationships:
  - Source: THETIS
  - Target: ACHILLES
  - Description: Thetis is the mother of Achilles, and her refusal to bear him would have prevented his existence, as referenced in the text.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
