    <node id="PHAROS">
      <data key="d0">PHAROS</data>
      <data key="d1">PHAROS</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">Pharos is a location referenced in classical literature, most notably in Ovid’s *Metamorphoses*, where it is identified as the lighthouse or island situated near Alexandria, Egypt. In this context, Pharos serves as a geographical and symbolic landmark, often evoking the famed lighthouse that guided sailors into the harbor. Additionally, Pharos is closely associated with the goddess Isis, as the text explicitly links the place to her worship and presence. This dual reference—both as a physical site of navigation and as a sacred space tied to Isis—highlights its significance in ancient Mediterranean culture, where it functioned as a point of convergence for maritime activity and religious devotion. The island of Pharos, historically connected to the Ptolemaic dynasty, was home to the famous lighthouse, one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World, and also housed a temple dedicated to Isis, underscoring its role as a center of both practical and spiritual life. Thus, Pharos emerges as a multifaceted entity: a real-world landmark, a literary motif, and a cultic site, all of which are woven into the narrative fabric of the *Metamorphoses*.
Relationships:
  - None listed in the provided data.</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
