    <node id="MISSOURI RIVER">
      <data key="d0">MISSOURI RIVER</data>
      <data key="d1">MISSOURI RIVER</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">The Missouri River is a significant geographical feature and historical location in the American landscape. It serves as a site where the remaining condemned Indians were eventually taken, and it was there that they were allowed to sow discontent, a situation that ultimately contributed to the Custer massacre. Additionally, the river is a notable landmark near Lexington, where General Price reached its banks during his military campaign and later retreated from the same position. Thus, the Missouri River is associated with both Native American displacement and military maneuvers, playing a role in two distinct historical events.
Relationships: (None listed in the relationships table)</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
