**ENTITY: WIGHT**
Description: Uncle Wight is a recurring relative in the narrator’s family, appearing in several key events and interactions. He is the narrator’s uncle, and his presence is noted across various domestic and legal occasions. One of the earliest mentions places him as a relative whose house the narrator visits, only to find him out. He is also involved in discussions about purchasing property at Brampton, and at one point, he dines with the narrator. During a visit to Mr. Rawlinson, Uncle Wight is out of town, and this absence is also noted at another time, confirming he was away from home on at least one occasion.

In a more specific family scene, Uncle Wight, along with the narrator’s father, left a hot house to cool off at an alehouse, indicating a casual, social relationship between the two brothers. He is also encountered at the Exchequer, where he meets the narrator, and later shares a meal with him. Uncle Wight is present at a will reading, attending by appointment, and actively participates in the subsequent family discussion regarding the estate. However, a point of tension arises when Uncle Wight becomes upset that the narrator’s father went to Brampton without informing him, suggesting that he values being kept in the loop on family matters and feels slighted by the lack of communication.

Overall, Uncle Wight is a close family member who is involved in both social and legal aspects of the narrator’s life. He is present at important family gatherings, such as the will reading, and engages in property discussions, but he also has a more personal side, as seen in his casual outing with the narrator’s father and his emotional reaction to being left out of a trip. His character is defined by his active participation in family affairs, his occasional absences, and his sensitivity to being excluded from decisions or plans. The narrator’s relationship with him appears to be one of familiarity and duty, with Uncle Wight serving as both a companion and a figure of familial expectation.
