    <node id="GEN. MCCLELLAN">
      <data key="d0">GEN. MCCLELLAN</data>
      <data key="d1">GEN. MCCLELLAN</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">George B. McClellan, commonly referred to as Gen. McClellan, was a prominent Union general during the American Civil War. He served as the commander of the Army of the Potomac, a position that made him a central figure in the Union's military efforts. McClellan was widely regarded as an idol of both the army and the American people, a status bolstered by his earlier career as the chief engineer of the Illinois Central railroad, which demonstrated his organizational and engineering skills. Despite his popularity, McClellan's tenure was marked by controversial decisions. He notably ordered the Hutchinson family, a well-known singing group, to be removed from the Army of the Potomac because they performed abolitionist songs, reflecting his cautious approach to the political dimensions of the war. Additionally, he issued an order for the arrest of fellow Union general Ulysses S. Grant, though this order was never carried out, as it was held up by higher authorities. These actions illustrate McClellan's sometimes contentious relationships with other military leaders and his sensitivity to political matters within the ranks. Overall, McClellan was a skilled organizer and a beloved leader among his troops, but his cautious strategies and political entanglements often put him at odds with the broader Union command. His legacy is a complex one, defined by both his early successes and his later controversies.
Relationships:
  - Source: GEN. GRANT
  - Target: GEN. MCCLELLAN
  - Description: McClellan ordered Grant's arrest, but the order was held up.

---</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
