    <node id="GEN. W.T. SHERMAN">
      <data key="d0">GEN. W.T. SHERMAN</data>
      <data key="d1">GEN. W.T. SHERMAN</data>
      <data key="d2">extracted</data>
      <data key="d3">GEN. W.T. SHERMAN, also known as William Tecumseh Sherman, was a prominent Union general during the American Civil War. He served as the commander of the Department of the Cumberland, a key military administrative division for the Union forces. In this capacity, he issued orders that directed the Second Minnesota regiment to proceed to Lebanon Junction, a strategic railroad point in Kentucky, indicating his role in troop movements and logistical coordination. Sherman was also a close friend and trusted confidant of General Ulysses S. Grant. During a period when Grant faced professional setbacks and was sidelined from active command, Sherman played a crucial personal and professional role by persuading Grant not to resign from the army. This intervention helped preserve Grant’s military career, which later proved vital to the Union’s ultimate victory. Sherman’s actions reflect his dual significance as both a field commander with operational responsibilities and a key figure in the Union’s high command, whose personal relationships and strategic counsel influenced the course of the war.
Relationships:
  - (No direct relationship listed in the data tables between GEN. GRANT and GEN. W.T. SHERMAN, but the description of GEN. W.T. SHERMAN mentions his relationship with Grant.)

---</data>
      <data key="d4">1</data>
      <data key="d5">0</data>
      <data key="d6">ENTITY: GEN. W.T. SHERMAN
Description: GEN. W.T. SHERMAN, also known as William Tecumseh Sherman, was a prominent Union general during the American Civil War. He served as the commander of the Department of the Cumberland, a key military administrative division for the Union forces. In this capacity, he issued orders that directed the Second Minnesota regiment to proceed to Lebanon Junction, a strategic railroad point in Kentucky, indicating his role in troop movements and logistical coordination. Sherman was also a close friend and trusted confidant of General Ulysses S. Grant. During a period when Grant faced professional setbacks and was sidelined from active command, Sherman played a crucial personal and professional role by persuading Grant not to resign from the army. This intervention helped preserve Grant’s military career, which later proved vital to the Union’s ultimate victory. Sherman’s actions reflect his dual significance as both a field commander with operational responsibilities and a key figure in the Union’s high command, whose personal relationships and strategic counsel influenced the course of the war.
Relationships:
  - (No direct relationship listed in the data tables between GEN. GRANT and GEN. W.T. SHERMAN, but the description of GEN. W.T. SHERMAN mentions his relationship with Grant.)

---</data>
    </node>
