    <edge source="RUDOLPH" target="GILLY" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">RUDOLPH</data>
      <data key="d8">GILLY</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Rudolph and Gilly are two individuals whose connection is established through their mutual association with an unnamed woman, though they are not directly related to each other in the text. Gilly is depicted as a companion who rides alongside Rudolph and the woman, and his presence adds weight to their conversations, suggesting he is a trusted and integral part of their dynamic. Rudolph confides in Gilly, sharing information about Bertha Forresters decision not to come to the town, indicating a level of trust and reliance on Gillys judgment. Furthermore, Rudolph entrusts Gilly with handling a difficult situation, underscoring Gillys capability and the strong bond of confidence between them. Together, Rudolph and Gilly operate as a cohesive unit, with Rudolph as the primary communicator and Gilly as a dependable ally in navigating challenges .</data>
      <data key="d12">RUDOLPH</data>
    </edge>
