    <edge source="HEYWOOD" target="MRS. FORRESTER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">HEYWOOD</data>
      <data key="d8">MRS. FORRESTER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Heywood and Mrs. Forrester are connected through a social encounter, likely at a dinner hosted by Heywood. Mrs. Forrester attends this event as a guest, and at some point during the evening, she moves to the garden. Heywood observes her there in the company of another individual named Chantel. His reaction to this sight is a gloomy stare, which strongly suggests disapproval of the situation or of Mrs. Forrester's association with Chantel. Additionally, Heywood expresses concern for Mrs. Forrester's well-being, specifically advising her not to go alone, implying he believes she may be at risk or that her safety could be compromised if she is unaccompanied. These details collectively paint a picture of Heywood as a protective or watchful host, while Mrs. Forrester is a guest whose actions draw his attention and concern. The exact nature of their relationshipwhether they are friends, acquaintances, or something moreis not specified, but Heywood's care and his disapproving gaze indicate a level of personal interest in her activities and safety.</data>
      <data key="d12">MRS. FORRESTER</data>
    </edge>
