    <edge source="SULTAN" target="EMPEROR" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SULTAN</data>
      <data key="d8">EMPEROR</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">SULTAN and EMPEROR are titles of sovereign authority, often used interchangeably in certain historical and cultural contexts. The Sultan is also referred to as the Emperor of Morocco, indicating that in this specific region, the two titles denote the same supreme ruler, combining Islamic and imperial connotations. In courtly tradition, a person who approaches the Emperor without offering a present is considered a menace or a source of discord, as the presentation of a gift functions as a formal greeting and a sign of respect. Thus, the act of giving is not merely ceremonial but essential to maintaining proper diplomatic and social relations with the sovereign. Together, these descriptions highlight the dual nature of the rulerboth as a Sultan with religious and political authority and as an Emperor with imperial statusand underscore the importance of gift-giving as a fundamental protocol in his court.</data>
      <data key="d12">EMPEROR</data>
    </edge>
