    <edge source="MY FATHER" target="BRAMPTON" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">MY FATHER</data>
      <data key="d8">BRAMPTON</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">My Father, a figure closely associated with the town of Brampton, is central to several interconnected events. He is actively involved in discussions about purchasing property or goods in Brampton, often alongside the narrator, indicating a shared financial or familial venture. These discussions suggest that My Father is a key decision-maker in matters related to Brampton, possibly considering an acquisition there. Additionally, he has traveled to Brampton to settle affairs, as noted in the context of Samuel Pepys's father, who is the same individual. This settlement implies that My Father has a formal or practical role in establishing a residence or managing business in the town. His return from Brampton further confirms that the town serves as a place of residence or travel for him, reinforcing his ongoing connection to the location. The descriptions collectively paint My Father as a person with significant ties to Brampton, engaging in purchasing discussions, undertaking settlement activities, and traveling between locations, all while maintaining a close relationship with the narrator, who is likely Samuel Pepys himself. There is no contradiction among the descriptions; rather, they complement each other, showing My Father's active involvement in Brampton's affairs, whether through property acquisition, settling, or regular visits. His actions suggest a practical and possibly long-term commitment to the town, with the narrator often accompanying or being informed of these activities. Thus, My Father emerges as a central figure whose movements and decisions regarding Brampton are integral to the narrative, highlighting his role as a family patriarch engaged in tangible, location-based responsibilities.</data>
      <data key="d12">MY FATHER</data>
    </edge>
