    <edge source="SAMUEL PEPYS" target="PALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">SAMUEL PEPYS</data>
      <data key="d8">PALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Samuel Pepys, the 17th-century English diarist and naval administrator, had a familial connection to a servant named Pall, who is mentioned in his records. Pall was a maid who remained in the Pepys household after the departure of another servant, Jane, and took on the full burden of the domestic work. Despite her continued service, Samuel Pepys discussed his intention not to keep Pall in his employ, indicating that she was not a permanent fixture in his household. The relationship between Pepys and Pall appears to be that of a master and a servant, though the mention of a "familial relationship" suggests that Pall may have been a relativepossibly a poor relation or a distant family memberwhom Pepys felt obligated to support temporarily but did not wish to retain long-term. This decision likely stemmed from practical or financial considerations, as Pepys was known for his meticulous management of his household and staff. Pall's role was significant enough to be noted in Pepys's writings, but her future in the household was uncertain, as Pepys clearly planned to let her go. The exact nature of their kinship is not fully detailed, but the context implies that Pall was not merely a hired servant but someone with a personal tie to the Pepys family, which may have complicated the decision to dismiss her. Ultimately, Pall's story is a brief but telling glimpse into the domestic arrangements and social hierarchies of Pepys's world, where even family members could serve in menial roles and be subject to the whims of the household head.</data>
      <data key="d12">PALL</data>
    </edge>
