    <edge source="GILLY" target="MRS. FORRESTER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">GILLY</data>
      <data key="d8">MRS. FORRESTER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">GILLY and MRS. FORRESTER are connected through a dynamic of protective responsibility and prior acquaintance. Gilly, a member of a group, demonstrates a clear sense of duty toward Mrs. Forrester, ensuring her safety and integration within their collective. When Mrs. Forrester enters, Gilly immediately reacts with a protest, calling out "Bertha!"a name that suggests a deeper, personal recognition of her, possibly indicating a shared history or an emotional bond that goes beyond mere group membership. This exclamation conveys both concern and familiarity, implying that Gilly is not only aware of her presence but also emotionally invested in her well-being. Furthermore, Gilly takes an active role in her care by instructing Rudolph to escort Mrs. Forrester. This action underscores Gillys authority or responsibility within the group, positioning him as a guardian or caretaker for her. The instruction to Rudolph indicates that Gilly cannot or will not personally escort her, but he ensures that she is accompanied and looked after, reflecting a structured approach to her safety. This responsibility suggests that Mrs. Forrester may be vulnerable, new, or in need of protection, and Gilly has taken it upon himself to oversee her welfare. Together, these descriptions paint a picture of a relationship marked by vigilance, care, and a personal connection. Gillys reaction to Mrs. Forresters arrival is not neutral; it is charged with emotion and recognition, while his subsequent directive shows practical, decisive action to keep her safe. Mrs. Forrester, for her part, appears to be the recipient of this attention, though her own perspective is not detailed. The use of the name "Bertha" suggests that Gilly knows her intimately, possibly from before their current circumstances, adding a layer of history to their interaction. In summary, Gilly is a protective figure who recognizes Mrs. Forrester (also called Bertha) and takes concrete steps to ensure her safety by delegating her escort to Rudolph. Their relationship is characterized by concern, familiarity, and a clear sense of responsibility on Gillys part, indicating that Mrs. Forrester holds a significant place in his considerations within the group dynamic .</data>
      <data key="d12">MRS. FORRESTER</data>
    </edge>
