    <edge source="GILLY" target="RUDOLPH" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">GILLY</data>
      <data key="d8">RUDOLPH</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Gilly and Rudolph are two individuals whose relationship is defined by both camaraderie and a clear hierarchical structure. They are first seen standing together under the stars, a moment that underscores their friendship and mutual presence. In this shared setting, they engage in a conversation about the presence of a boat and the broader situation they find themselves in, indicating that they are both aware of and actively discussing the circumstances around them. However, their dynamic is not purely egalitarian. Gilly holds a position of authority over Rudolph, as evidenced by Gilly issuing a direct order for Rudolph to escort Mrs. Forrester. This command reveals a structured group hierarchy in which Gilly has the power to delegate tasks, while Rudolph is expected to follow instructions. Despite this formal power imbalance, their initial scene together suggests a foundation of trust and familiarity, allowing them to work in tandem even as Gilly takes the lead in decision-making. Thus, Gilly and Rudolph share a bond of friendship, yet their interactions are also shaped by a defined chain of command, with Gilly acting as the superior and Rudolph as the subordinate. Their combined presence under the stars and their discussion of the boat indicate that they are both integral to the groups ongoing situation, with Gilly directing actions and Rudolph carrying them out .</data>
      <data key="d12">RUDOLPH</data>
    </edge>
