      <data key="d11">Constantine is a village on the upland road from Falmouth, visible from high ground</data>
      <data key="d12">FALMOUTH</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FALMOUTH" target="MABE" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">FALMOUTH</data>
      <data key="d8">MABE</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Falmouth and Mabe are two closely situated locations in Cornwall, England, with a clear geographic relationship. Mabe is a village located near Falmouth, and it is visible from the high ground on the route between the two places. Specifically, from elevated points near Falmouthsuch as the high ground along the connecting roadMabe can be seen in the distance. This visibility underscores their proximity and the open, rolling landscape that separates them. Both entities are distinct settlements, but their close spatial connection makes Mabe a notable landmark when traveling or viewing the area from Falmouths higher elevations.</data>
      <data key="d12">FALMOUTH</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="FALMOUTH" target="CORNWALL" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">FALMOUTH</data>
      <data key="d8">CORNWALL</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Falmouth is a town located in the county of Cornwall, in the southwestern part of England. It serves as a key settlement within Cornwalls coastal landscape, situated along the countys shoreline. The town is notable as the endpoint of a journey, likely referring to a railway route or travel narrative, and is part of the broader coastal geography that defines the region. In one account, a railway friend issued a warning about being "taken in" at Falmouth, suggesting a cautionary tale or local anecdote about the town, possibly relating to scams, deception, or an unexpected experience. Despite this warning, Falmouth remains a recognized town within Cornwall, integrated into the countys maritime and transport context. The descriptions consistently identify Falmouth as a town in Cornwall, with no contradictions, and collectively emphasize its geographic location, its role as a journeys end, and a specific cautionary remark from a railway acquaintance. Thus, Falmouth is a coastal Cornish town, historically and practically linked to rail travel, and the subject of a local warning about being deceived or misled upon arrival.</data>
      <data key="d12">FALMOUTH</data>
    </edge>
