    <edge source="ISIS" target="OSIRIS" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">ISIS</data>
      <data key="d8">OSIRIS</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">ISIS and OSIRIS are central figures in ancient Egyptian mythology, deeply intertwined through kinship, worship, and ritual. Isis is identified as the wife and sister of Osiris, establishing a divine familial bond that is both intimate and sacred. Osiris, in turn, is a deity whose worship is closely linked to that of Isis, as referenced in various texts and religious practices. Their relationship is further emphasized in the rites dedicated to them, particularly in the search for Osiriss limbsa mythological event that underscores the themes of death, dismemberment, and restoration. This ritual search is a key element of their shared cult, highlighting Isiss role as a devoted consort who seeks to reassemble and revive her husband. Additionally, the texts note that Osiris loves Isiss rites, indicating a reciprocal dynamic of divine love and devotion. This mutual affection is not merely personal but is woven into the fabric of their worship, where the performance of rituals honors both deities and reflects their enduring bond. Together, ISIS and OSIRIS represent a powerful mythological pair, embodying concepts of resurrection, loyalty, and the sacred union of divine forces. Their story and associated rites form a cornerstone of Egyptian religious tradition, illustrating how their relationshipboth as siblings and spousesshapes the spiritual landscape of the culture. The descriptions collectively portray a harmonious and deeply connected divine partnership, where Isiss actions and Osiriss love for her rites reinforce their inseparable nature. No contradictions arise among the provided details; instead, they complement one another, painting a coherent picture of a mythic couple whose worship and mythology are mutually reinforcing. Thus, ISIS and OSIRIS stand as enduring symbols of love, loss, and renewal, their narrative and rituals continuing to resonate through history.</data>
      <data key="d12">ISIS</data>
    </edge>
