    <edge source="GALLI" target="CORYBANTES" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">GALLI</data>
      <data key="d8">CORYBANTES</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">The Galli and the Corybantes are two distinct groups of priests connected through their shared devotion to the goddess Cybele, the ancient Phrygian mother goddess. The Corybantes were the original priests of Cybele, known for their ecstatic, frenzied rites, which included loud music, dancing, and the clashing of shields and spears. The Galli, who served as Cybeles priests in later periods, deliberately imitated the Corybantes in their religious practices. This imitation was not merely superficial but reflected a deep religious connection, as the Galli adopted the Corybantes ecstatic and often self-castrating rituals, along with their use of tambourines, cymbals, and other percussive instruments. The Gallis rites were thus a continuation and adaptation of the Corybantic tradition, preserving the core elements of Cybeles worship while evolving in different cultural contexts. Both groups were central to the goddesss cult, and their shared practices underscore a direct lineage of ritualistic expression, with the Galli serving as the later inheritors of the Corybantes sacred duties.</data>
      <data key="d12">GALLI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GALLI" target="PIPES" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">GALLI</data>
      <data key="d8">PIPES</data>
