    <edge source="JACK FLEMING" target="KATINKA JALLINGER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">JACK FLEMING</data>
      <data key="d8">KATINKA JALLINGER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Jack Fleming and Katinka Jallinger share a personal and mutually supportive relationship, as evidenced by their interactions. Their connection begins with a friendly encounter in which Jack meets Katinka, borrows her pan, and assists her in fixing her dress, demonstrating a practical and amicable rapport. This initial meeting establishes a tone of cooperation and goodwill between them. Subsequently, Katinka deepens their bond by writing a personal letter to Jack and sending him gold flakes, a gesture that signifies trust, affection, and a meaningful personal connection. Together, these actions indicate that their relationship evolves from a casual acquaintance into a more intimate and valued friendship, marked by kindness, reciprocity, and thoughtful communication.</data>
      <data key="d12">KATINKA JALLINGER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="JACK FLEMING" target="HENRY BOONE JALLINGER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">JACK FLEMING</data>
      <data key="d8">HENRY BOONE JALLINGER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Jack Fleming is aware of Katinka's father, Henry Boone Jallinger, and mentions him in conversation.</data>
      <data key="d12">HENRY BOONE JALLINGER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="JACK FLEMING" target="TINKA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">JACK FLEMING</data>
      <data key="d8">TINKA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">JACK FLEMING and TINKA are central figures in a shared narrative, connected through a significant personal and material exchange. According to the provided descriptions, Fleming gave a pan to Tinka, an act that is explicitly mentioned in a letter, indicating a deliberate and meaningful gesture between them. This gift is not merely transactional but is deeply tied to their romantic relationship. The two are romantically involved, and their bond is further solidified through a specific action: Tinka salted the pana process of adding gold dust or nuggets to a mining pan to create the illusion of a rich findwhich she did with the intention of bringing Fleming back to her. This act of salting the pan was a strategic and affectionate move, designed to reunite them. Following the gold discovery that resulted from this salted pan, the couple becomes engaged, marking a pivotal turning point in their relationship. Thus, the pan serves as a catalyst for both their reunion and their formal commitment to one another, intertwining themes of love, deception, and fortune. The narrative highlights how a simple object, the pan, becomes a symbol of their connection, leading to a happy resolution in their personal lives.</data>
      <data key="d12">TINKA</data>
    </edge>
