      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">COWS and MANURE HEAP are closely associated elements within a pasture field environment. The cows are positioned around or near the manure heap, which is a natural part of the field's landscape. The cows are actively engaged in cropping grass, indicating they are grazing in the immediate vicinity of the manure heap. This setting places the cows and the manure heap in a shared, open agricultural space, where the manure heap serves as a fixed feature of the field. The cows' presence near the heap does not appear to be for feeding on the manure itself, but rather they are grazing on the surrounding grass. The manure heap is thus an integral component of the field's environment, and the cows' proximity to it is a normal part of their daily activity in the pasture. No contradictory information is present; both descriptions consistently depict the cows as being close to the manure heap while grazing.</data>
      <data key="d12">MANURE HEAP</data>
    </edge>
