    <edge source="OVID" target="EPISTLE OF HERO TO LEANDER" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">OVID</data>
      <data key="d8">EPISTLE OF HERO TO LEANDER</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Ovid is the author of the Epistle of Hero to Leander, as referenced in the text.</data>
      <data key="d12">OVID</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="OVID" target="PONTIC EPISTLES" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">OVID</data>
      <data key="d8">PONTIC EPISTLES</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Ovid, the Roman poet, is the author of the *Pontic Epistles* (also known as the *Epistulae ex Ponto*), a collection of verse letters written during his exile. These epistles are referenced in the text as a key work of his later career. Among the recipients of these letters is Grcinus, to whom several of the *Pontic Epistles* are specifically addressed. The collection reflects Ovids personal sorrow, appeals for leniency, and observations on life in exile, and it stands as a significant part of his literary legacy.</data>
      <data key="d12">OVID</data>
    </edge>
