    <edge source="ABD-EL-KADER" target="SULTAN" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">ABD-EL-KADER</data>
      <data key="d8">SULTAN</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Abd-el-Kader was a prominent military leader who served under the Sultan, commanding a force whose infantry was considered reliable and dependable in combat. However, despite this service, a significant conflict arose between the two figures. Abd-el-Kader ultimately resolved to challenge the Sultan directly, seeking to test his own strength against his superior in a pitched battle. This decision marked a clear rupture in their relationship, transforming from one of subordinate command to open rivalry. The descriptions indicate that while Abd-el-Kader was a capable chief with trustworthy infantry, his ambition or grievances led him to confront the Sultan militarily, rather than continue in a subordinate role. The exact reasons for this break are not specified, but the outcome was a direct military confrontation between the two leaders.</data>
      <data key="d12">ABD-EL-KADER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ABD-EL-KADER" target="ALGERIA" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">ABD-EL-KADER</data>
      <data key="d8">ALGERIA</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Abd-el-Kader was obliged to retire from Algeria, indicating a political relationship.</data>
      <data key="d12">ABD-EL-KADER</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="ABD-EL-KADER" target="MOROCCO" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">ABD-EL-KADER</data>
      <data key="d8">MOROCCO</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Abd-el-Kader is a figure of profound reverence in Morocco, a nation historically regarded as the "terre classique" (classic land) of holy-fighting men, or marabout warriors. Within this context, Abd-el-Kader is honored as a marabout warrior, a status that elevates him beyond mere military leadership to a spiritual and religious icon. His veneration in Morocco is deeply intertwined with the country's cultural and religious identity, where the tradition of combining martial prowess with sanctity is highly esteemed. Furthermore, Abd-el-Kader is noted for uniting princely and sacerdotal (priestly) authority, a characteristic he shares with the Shereefsdescendants of the Prophet Muhammad who hold both political and religious leadership in Morocco. This dual role positions him as a significant figure in the governance and spiritual life of the region, as he embodies the fusion of temporal power and religious legitimacy. His association with Morocco's governance underscores his influence, as he is seen not only as a warrior but also as a leader who bridges the secular and the sacred, much like the Shereefs who have historically shaped Moroccan statehood.</data>
      <data key="d12">ABD-EL-KADER</data>
    </edge>
