    <edge source="CYBELE" target="GALLI" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CYBELE</data>
      <data key="d8">GALLI</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Cybele is the ancient Phrygian mother goddess, and the Galli are her priests, serving as the primary officiants of her cult. The Galli are specifically dedicated to performing the rites and rituals associated with Cybeles worship, which were central to her religious practices. Their role is defined by a direct religious connection to the goddess, as they acted as her ordained servants and intermediaries between the deity and her devotees. The Gallis duties encompassed the ceremonial aspects of Cybeles cult, including processions, music, and ecstatic rites, which were characteristic of her worship in the ancient world. As priests, the Galli held a distinct and revered position within the religious hierarchy, embodying the devotion and ritual observance required to honor Cybele. Their identity and function are inseparable from the goddess, as they were the primary agents through which her rites were conducted. Thus, the Galli are fundamentally the priests of Cybele, tasked with the performance of her sacred ceremonies and the maintenance of her cultic traditions.</data>
      <data key="d12">GALLI</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="CYBELE" target="CORYBANTES" id="related_to">
      <data key="d7">CYBELE</data>
      <data key="d8">CORYBANTES</data>
      <data key="d9">related_to</data>
      <data key="d10">1.0</data>
      <data key="d11">Cybele, the ancient Phrygian mother goddess, was served by a distinct class of male priests known as the Corybantes. These priests were the original religious officiants of her cult, predating and serving as the model for the later Galli, who were the eunuch priests associated with Cybeles more ecstatic and self-castrating rites. The Corybantes performed their duties in a state of frenzied worship, often characterized by loud music, dancing, and the clashing of shields and spears, which were believed to drive away evil spirits and induce a state of divine possession. Their role was central to the mystery cult of Cybele, and their rituals were later imitated and adapted by the Galli, who took on a more extreme form of devotion, including voluntary castration and the wearing of female attire. The connection between the Corybantes and the Galli highlights a religious lineage, where the original priests set the ceremonial and spiritual foundation that the Galli would later follow, albeit with modifications. Thus, the Corybantes are historically significant as the earliest known priests of Cybele, establishing the core practices and theological framework of her worship, which the Galli perpetuated and transformed in later periods.</data>
      <data key="d12">CORYBANTES</data>
    </edge>
